Yes — as a systems model, that’s much closer to reality than the usual one-way “brain commands body” picture.

I’d tighten it into a multi-timescale feedback loop:

perception/thought → appraisal → action/posture/breath/movement → interoceptive feedback → emotion/autonomic state → endocrine signaling → altered attention/motivation/energy → new thought/action

Hormones are not merely outputs at the end. They are state variables that feed back into the controller. Cortisol, adrenaline, testosterone, thyroid hormones, insulin, oxytocin, sex hormones, etc. can change salience, vigilance, appetite, confidence, social behavior, memory, fatigue, and therefore which thoughts and actions become probable next.

Your “body language to your emotions” phrasing has a real physiological analogue: the brain continuously reads the body through interoception and proprioception. Breathing pattern, muscle tension, facial expression, heart rate, movement, and posture contribute evidence about what state the organism is in. The body is both actuator and sensor.

I’d separate epigenetics by timescale, though. It usually isn’t:

emotion → epigenetics immediately tells gland which hormone to make.

It is more like repeated hormonal, neural, metabolic, environmental, and behavioral conditions can alter gene regulation over longer periods, which can then change how strongly parts of the system respond in the future.

So you get stacked loops:

milliseconds–seconds: neural signaling
seconds–minutes: autonomic + fast endocrine changes
hours–days: hormonal regulation, receptor changes, metabolism
days–years: learning, plasticity, gene-expression and some epigenetic adaptation

That makes your “higher octave” metaphor useful: the same basic sense → respond → sense consequence → adapt pattern keeps reappearing at slower and slower biological scales.

Or in Pantheon shorthand:

thought moves body → body reports state → state tunes chemistry → chemistry biases thought → repetition tunes the organism.

Not mind over body or body over mind.

One coupled instrument playing itself.